{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/268433-fixing-changeling","id":268433,"title":"'Fixing' changeling","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Changeling: The Dreaming"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-09-29T12:12:14-07:00","id":268433,"title":"'Fixing' changeling","content":"I love changeling. I really do. But as written, it's quite difficult to explain to new people (What do you mean two souls?), the powers are kinda flat (So I get a facelift and forget I'm a fairy?), gathering glamour is really difficult (JUST BE INSPIRED) and the dreaming is impossible to explain (What do you mean I see both?). With that in mind, what ideas have people got for streamlining changeling, making glamour less difficult to get (not even easy, just less painful), making cantrips more equal to gifts/disciplines and making it easier on first timers?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>-Failure is always an option<br /> roseerifnosi","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/115-dahqueendiva","name":"DahQueenDiva","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-29T12:59:56-07:00","id":268478,"title":"","content":"I don't play Changeling so without knowing the exact rules I offer doing something like a hybrid of Gnosis and Willpower. To regain Glamour have them choose a craft at character creation. It could be art, acting, writing, music, engineering, martial arts, smithing, what ever; this becomes the thing that inspires them the most. Then follow the rules for meditation. For every hour they spend doing what ever inspires them, let them make an Attribute+Ability roll to see if they gain any Glamour. This could also include anytime they get to apply their given craft during a scene in some strange way or if they see someone else do something that fits into their craft into a new way they have never seen or done before. It should be appropriate to their character concept and require ST approval.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1619-derzhuzad","name":"Derzhuzad","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1619_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"652"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-29T13:03:32-07:00","id":268483,"title":"","content":"I kinda feel like Changeling, more than the other lines, needs more reading to get into. I had less of a problem with the mechanics, and more trouble just getting myself into the setting. The (1st edition) core book I have puts a huge emphasis on establishing tone, but spends less time showing you what the world is actually like. The time spent on tone is lovely! It's a huge draw, and it really sets the line apart from others...but I think it just needs more time spent talking about what everyday life is like for PCs. Everyday life is key! You can extrapolate grand quests from everyday life, but not vice versa. Writing about epic showdowns and glorious battles is all well and good but that's not what seems lacking.<br /> <br /> I didn't have too bad of a time with some of the concepts you mention, like the dual natures...the bigger problem I had is that, whenever we tried getting a game together, all the players were real excited to play their characters, but the GMs didn't have much idea what the world was going to look like around them. PCs were well established but they didn't know how to act within the world.<br /> <br /> I'd say just more stories. Pages and pages of them even, and not &quot;example of gameplay&quot; with dice rolling examples. Whenever there was a big story in a Changeling book I came out of it with a better sense of both setting and mechanics and what I was supposed to &quot;do&quot;. Actually, I think the prose did help with the existing-in-both-worlds thing, and probably stories dealing with inspiring glamour, or things like how that &quot;freeholds are entirely in the Dreaming even though you can just walk in from the street&quot; thing works, would help a lot.<br /> <br /> Lines like Werewolf are easy to get into without knowing what you're doing. Okay I don't know how a sept is really put together well let's throw you at an evil oil refinery and there's ugly things attacking you. Even then, understanding the Umbra just isn't possible without solid fiction written in it, not just gameplay examples. It helps that Werewolf is kinda &quot;pop culture werewolves plus a spirit world&quot;. Changeling on the other hand feels like... &quot;okay, watch Neverwhere, except you live in the real world and the Dreaming at the same time, and there's Glamour, and it's usually less terrifying and more wondrous, and in any case you usually have different thing you're afraid of and you spend your time completely differently&quot;--it's more steps apart from what we are already used to.<br /> <br /> So cram more prose into the book, and if people complain &quot;Changeling has too much writing and not enough crunch,&quot; just nod your head and say &quot;well yeah it's Changeling, that's what it's like&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2214,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2214-traveller","user":"Traveller","date":". 09-29-2014, 04:05 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2214-traveller","name":"Traveller","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2214_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-29T14:12:14-07:00","id":268526,"title":"","content":"I don't think CtD needs more prose. It needs better prose. It definitely suffered worse than the other game lines for having purple prose over useful prose.<br /> <br /> It is is, frankly, a game. If it needs more prose to communicate itself (and it has plenty) something is kind of wrong with it. Games are meant to be played, not just read and put on a shelf. It is there for us to tell stories with, not to be passive about.<br /> <br /> And no, the mechanical problems with Changeling are not fixed by trying to explain them more. There are definitely concepts which need better writing (Banality and the two-worlds thing), but it is still a case of quality not quantity. If the concepts cannot be adequately communicated in the book the size of CtD's core book (either) than the concepts need to be reexamined to see if they're too much concept and not enough RPG tool. Changeling should not be defended ala Chinatown. For example M20 did actually address tightening up the prose on core concepts (the new Focus model vs. the old paradigm/foci model) and getting them across more effectively. They didn't leave it as just, &quot;well that's how Mage is,&quot; and M20 seems to be better it (at least from the Quickstart).<br /> <br /> There are also mechanics that need work. There can be (and has been) debate on what they should be based on what different people want out of the game, but that doesn't change the fact that the current rules have lots of areas where the results are not what the books aim for.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-30T18:54:53-07:00","id":269705,"title":"","content":"I think the big problem is that it's not that intuitive because of a disconnect between the faerie tales people know and how it relates to the society and character creation in the setting. Another thing is that while vampire, werewolf, and mage can allow people to live vicariously through their cool characters with powers beyond mortal ken (even if at a price), it's much harder for people to see how being a changeling can do that. I can explain the other lines in one or two sentences, and people will want to play. Changeling seems to require an hour long discussion which cause most people to just bail out and never try it.<br /> <br /> 1) Make it easier for people to mirror actual myth and legend. Too often, Changeling made it hard for people to determine how to duplicate faerie tale character X in the game if they were inspired by it; a related problem is that instead of using the Kiths as broad archetypes, they increasingly started assigning very specific Kiths to do it. The game would have benefited by giving actual examples of real faerie tales and what Kith would fit. One can figure that out on one's own, but it should be much easier if you want to bring in new players into the game. I still have trouble figuring out what the hell an Eshu is supposed to be in terms of real life faerie tales. There is no reason why so many partial animal faeries have their own splat, Pooka should be enough except they defined it too strictly as &quot;trickster&quot; so all the other animal type faeries (huldras, selkies, swanmays) are excluded.<br /> <br /> 2) It is hard for new players to know what the hell their character is supposed to do. It is much more apparent in other game lines because people know how vampires, werewolves, etc. work as protagonists in fiction, and they can easily imagine &quot;What would I do if were Lestat, Larry Talbot, Jim Dresden, etc.&quot;. On the other hand, faerie tales weren't written with the idea people would identify with the faeries. Instead, we have Narnia or some of the works of Neil Gaiman or Mervyn Peake. However, while people can understand that you are playing the role of Lucy, Peter, Edmund, etc. in Narnia, it's much harder to figure why they should do so rather than just play D&amp;D. CtD needs to do a much better job leading new players to the water.<br /> <br /> 3) The corebook lacked much of the setting conflict that was established in supplemental material. So once again it is harder for new players (and STs) to decide how a chronicle should look, what hooks they could use, etc.<br /> <br /> 4) Do a better job integrating the real world and real people with the fantastic of the setting. Too much essentially takes players out of the real world into its own corner rather than integrating it. Once things become too divorced from normality, a lot of players will decide they'd rather just play an outright fantasy campaign.<br /> <br /> I actually think that along the entire game line, these elements are in there. They just need to presented in their entirety and integrated much better in any new corebook.<br /> <br /> \u200bI speak as someone who has looked at the line repeatedly, but always decided to not play it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-30T21:43:56-07:00","id":269830,"title":"","content":"I'm looking forward to C20. I could never get into it before for the very reasons you cite, and I'm fully expecting them to fix that stuff.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3823-m_strauss","name":"M_Strauss","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"41"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-04T09:50:35-07:00","id":272314,"title":"","content":"I did run it a couple of times. Once to fulfill the wishes of a long time player and the other to supplement another game, Mage, where we cut away about once a year to run a short (month or less) side-story showing what was going on with the other supernaturals in the setting. <br /> <br /> However, I do agree with a lot of your concerns. And with Heavy Arms comments above.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-07T01:19:48-08:00","id":362223,"title":"","content":"I think Heavy Arms and Black Fox have good points. It's not that C:tD needs more prose, it's that it needs better prose, As well as the rules not being very intuitive.<br /> <br /> When Wendell_Burke and I had a chance to discuss some of the mechanics, it became clear that Changeling does seem to have everything one would need to play the game well in the main core book (2nd ed), however it's just written very haphazardly. All the gems to explain certain abilities or mechanics lie buried in other supplements. What the Changeling: the Dreaming core book really needs is for those refinements and exposition that explains gameplay best, to be consolidated into one source. I still have trouble understanding Changeling. Apparently that clears up according to other long time changeling players&gt; I've been told time and again that if I don't &quot;get it&quot; I just haven't read the entire series of books enough times to figure out how it all fits together.<br /> <br /> There's lots of other threads debating ways changeling could be improved and things we hope Changeling the 20th Anniversary edition will grant us. However I think the best we can do to make those changes happen, is to discuss aspects of Changeling's system in depth in as many different ways as possible, so that the developers can read these threads and see just where the issues lie. It shouldn't be a lambasting of the system, but a thorough dissection and critique of what mechanic works, what writing style needs to explain better, where the contradictions in the rules sit forgotten. It isn't enough to say Changeling needs <i>Fixing</i>, we'll have to roll up our sleeves and start examining every word, getting down to the nitty gritty so the way to 'fixing' Changeling can be made clear to those doing the research and the writing can see what we, the community think makes the most sense.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/19/w20supporterheavymetal1.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/31/25ew.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/829/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><a href=\"http://glyphery.webs.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://i.imgur.com/Ut0SZ4h.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1332-darling_rose","name":"Darling_Rose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1332_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"434"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-15T12:37:35-08:00","id":371350,"title":"","content":"I, for one, found C:tD to be my first love in the World of Darkness. It sparked me being a huge fan of the Fair Folk in Exalted, just due to the tenuous link in backstory.<br /> <br /> However, mechanically, not only is it clunky and poorly organized, but honestly, I don't think the people who wrote the character creation rules read the rules for how to actually use the powers. Glamour is too hard to gather, the realms require far too much investment, and the arts are...well, kind-of narrow. That last is more an artifact of how most WoD splats were handled, and wouldn't be a problem on its own. But the Realms... it was nearly impossible to have enough to be able to use your powers on much of anything.<br /> <br /> Strangely (for those who know me), I don't have a broad proposal for fixing the problems like how hard Glamour-management is. Realms...I like the concept. Honestly, that might be as easy as just giving more dots of them.<br /> <br /> Tone-wise, it's very cool. There was a time I was unsure whether you were really playing anything but a guy who shared his hallucinations with others, because I couldn't tell if the Changelings could really do much supernatural in the real world. But re-examination said they theoretically could. Just, you know, not with the resources given as written.<br /> <br /> I think C20 would be stronger than its predecessor if the Dreaming had more impact on the real world, and had a surreal, haunting influence that reaches out to touch the other supernatural creatures. Make them somehow...feel that a Changeling is bringing a lot of invisible power to bear. So using Auspex or Mage Sight or werewolf senses to perceive the Dreaming, when appropriate, is both chilling (suddenly, how much threat Changelings pose might be visible), and mildly necessary (so the threat isn't invisible as well as present).<br /> <br /> That said, Changelinges have always been the most fragile of the splats, and I'm not sure I'd like to see that change. But it'd be nice to have a solid indication that the dual worlds are there for more than just them, and that their ability to draw on the Dreaming makes them serious players in the supernatural world. Tying the mundane world to the Dreaming's reflections is how you render them vulnerable in both: they're just mortals, in the World of Darkness, but the World of Darkness impacts the Dreaming in strange and terrible ways.<br /> <br /> Sorry, mostly rambling. I love the splat, and it is the anniversary edition I am looking forward to the most. I hope it's fully playable, this time!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/827-segev","name":"Segev","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1001"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-17T12:31:18-08:00","id":373671,"title":"","content":"This is mainly the reason I ignore Changling and use Dark Ages: Fae instead.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1776-sidious","name":"Sidious","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1776_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"92"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-17T17:29:32-08:00","id":373933,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sidious</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/268433-fixing-changeling?p=373671#post373671\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is mainly the reason I ignore Changling and use Dark Ages: Fae instead.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's what I was about to say, &quot;Changeling Fixed&quot; would be DA: Fae....<br /> <br /> Now if they would do a modern incarnation of DA: Fae instead of C20 would be awesome.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1997-deathpathoes","name":"DeathPathoes","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"38"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-19T06:33:56-08:00","id":375217,"title":"","content":"I know nothing about Dark Ages: Fae. What can you tell me about it and why does it fix C:tD?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/827-segev","name":"Segev","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1001"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-19T09:47:17-08:00","id":375324,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure DA Fae really &quot;fixes&quot; CtD as much as it is a radically different game. It's not CtD Rev. with a whole lot of spit and polish applied to the old rules to make them shine. It's a completely separate approach (IMO close to how Exalted handles them really). I think a lot of people like it better because it's far more cohesive, is at least very cognizant and up front on when something that could have been mechanically defined wasn't for the players/ST to make up themselves, the mechanics are generally cleaner, the game handles the two-worlds issue and generally feeling like a game about fae more successfully, and massively kicks the CtD splat-bload issue to the curb.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-19T12:41:09-08:00","id":375470,"title":"","content":"Dark Ages: Fae is basically NWoD Changling before it came out. DA:Fae inspired a lot of the NWoD and Exalted stuff so I'm told. It's a very well done set of rules that are way more fun to play than Changling and fit the World of Darkness far more.<br /> <br /> Basically, Fae in it are monsters of old and are utterly inhuman. They are weird and strange, and make contracts with humans. The humans are slowly eroding their world with their static concepts and the Fae are being driven into hiding. Fae are immortal and some predate time, and time itself is fickle to a Fae. So very fickle. They don't perceive it at a constant rate as a human does. Your character is always at constant threat of gaining new weaknesses too. So yeah, it's pretty fun and way more WoD-y than CtD. I always recommend it to ST's for making Fae over CtD.<br /> <br /> Good luck finding a hard copy, the book was a really small batch when released and as Ian W will tell you, it was rare as all hell afterwards ($70 for the $20 book was a deal after launch). As always, the Dark Ages line makes the way better rules and fluff than the original modern nights stuff, but they require patching for a Modern game if you want to use them.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1VN49Y7tH2ZV0l7Hx65RWX1ez-Q9Lst-8ZTyTJeCTpxM/\" target=\"_blank\">Sorcerer 20th</a> homebrew and my update to <a href=\"http://immortalthegathering.blogspot.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Highlander: the Gathering</a> for 20th Anniversary edition.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2414-theoutlander523","name":"theoutlander523","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2414_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"238"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-19T15:07:36-08:00","id":375613,"title":"","content":"While the DA Revised is, to me, still a better base rule set than even the 20th books so far, I'm not sure the individual supernatural power mechanics are entirely good. The DAF rules being better than CtD isn't saying that much, and there are some pretty serious issues you can run into. Something that DA Mage and DA Fae did was compress things down to extremely small number of traits (in fact, both use four 1-5 traits to cover pretty much everything) which can be a bit of a pain to run. Both games have some very strong points in them. The whole Echoes deal where the Fae are weak to various folklore wards against them is one of the best weakness setups in the cWOD... but at the same time it relies a large amount on the ST and players to define them; and the guidance could be better.<br /> <br /> DA Mage and DA Fae both suffer a lot from not being &quot;new user&quot; friendly. Despite smooth rules, they rely a bit too heavily on people used to pre-existing games (esp. Mage) to work their best.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-21T06:30:17-08:00","id":377145,"title":"","content":"Hm. I wonder if it would be fun, in this thread, to really try to analyze what did and did not work in C:tD, what flavor elements it evoked well and which its mechanics hindered, and discuss ways to re-evaluate the mechanics in a broad sense to better capture that feel. (Admittedly, this is a mental exercise I like to do with most games, so...I'm biased.)<br /> <br /> For me, the biggest flaw in the mechanical execution of C:tD is that, in order for it to feel like you're playing a supernatural creature tragically trapped, rather than merely a delusional mortal with whom other mortals with similar delusions play SCA events, you have to have high levels in all the Realms, and a ton of Glamour. Because Glamour is ludicrously hard to recover, you need a large pool (and thus to be a Childling) to really have any freedom to be a supernatural creature even weekly.<br /> <br /> There also was little reason to play an older Changeling. A Wilder or a Grump got next to nothing but downside. Banality was not the double-edged sword it was apparently meant to be, and the war between the two sides (human and fae) to try to keep your sanity as both tended to leave little reward for risking falling into madness on the fae side (losing touch with mortality). There just wasn't enough oomph in being a beautiful mad faerie to make it tempting.<br /> <br /> There was also a very seductive, but unfortunately flavor-violating, tendency to treat Banality - as used by the antagonists - as an almost &quot;dark magic&quot; compared to the &quot;good&quot; magic (in relative, subjective-to-the-splat terms) of Glamour. By this, I mean that, while Banality for player characters tended to just be a bad thing, or to be something they could occasionally use to shake off the imaginary-pretend-games effects of Changeling magical duels (because, again, the division between worlds was TOO strong, so it felt like it really was more playing imaginary games than being a supernatural creature in the world of darkness)...while Banality was just bad stuff and hurt-yourself-for-armor for PCs, for NPCs it fueled actual supernatural effects.<br /> <br /> I think it would have been better served if Banality were something that enabled Fae to overcome their Echos-style weaknesses, to use those weaknesses in a mortal fashion against each other, and, where used as a &quot;dark magic,&quot; it looks to the practitioner like just being...banal. Mundane. The &quot;banality as dark magic&quot; portrayal can work from the perspective of those given over to the madness of the Fae; it can look to them like a deep mystery. But it shouldn't feel that way to the practitioner...and it unfortunately still kind-of looked like it.<br /> <br /> I think a tug-of-war of useful, tempting capabilities drawn from both halves of their nature - Banality for their humanity and Glamour for their fae selves - would have evoked the game's tone more powerfully.<br /> <br /> To achieve this, Banality would have to be more than just a &quot;nope&quot; effect when used positively. The idea that Grumps have more of it than any other Changeling (while remaining, well, Changelings) should have given them interesting ways to bring mortal protections to bear in the Dreaming. It should be something that lets them strike the balance, at least in appearance, more readily. Perhaps starting Grumps actually are more mortal than fae, as they are so mired in Banality with so little experience with Glamour that they do face the actual struggle. But being more worldly, more Banal, should also be its own advantage.<br /> <br /> Grumps should have to struggle to NOT use these advantages in order to advance in Glamour.<br /> <br /> Childlings, on the other hand, should actually be in the most danger of losing themselves to their faerie nature entirely. Less connected to the world, they should find that the Echoes really do bind them strongly. The more they draw on their Faerie nature, the more Fae they should become to mortal eyes, as well: Remember, that little boy houes the ancient soul of a Nocker mastercraftsman. If he's come too strongly into his Fae nature, it's not with the eyes of a human child that he looks upon the mortal world, but the eyes of an ancient being that views the world through an alien lens.<br /> <br /> Childlings too swept up in Glamour and not rooted in Banality should come across as, well, fey. They're too old and wise for their years, too obsessed with the mania of their fae archetype (the Nocker, for instance, may already be working on adult crafts such as instrument-making, automobile repair, or artistry of their own), and to almost paradoxically be obsessed with childish taboos like not stepping on cracks or pretending the floor is lava or never, EVER letting their clothes be inside-out.<br /> <br /> Banality, when it hits a Childling, actually helps them be a <i>child</i>. At the same time, it helps them grow up. It protects them from the monsters even as it makes them more afraid of them. (The Troll Dame in the Chlldling body fears no monster in the dark...but the little girl when reminded that she's just a child who sees monsters under her bed might doubt her faerie powers, and find refuge in Daddy's assurances that there are no such things as monsters. Even if she doesn't really believe it.)<br /> <br /> Grumps can threaten to disbelieve, wielding their Banality as a two-edged sword and struggling to accept their Glamour-ridden sight. Willfully ACCEPTING the reality of the Dreaming renders them vulnerable in ways they prefer not to be, but lets them work with it and gain power over it. For the Grump, it's a question of whether they tap this power of faerie magic at the risk of growing madness.<br /> <br /> In a sense, I suppose what I propose is that the ability to use Glamour in the real world come with Banality, if only because it's what one needs to perceive the world as it is and recognize that it is the Dreaming that is an imposition. Meanwhile, power within the Dreaming comes with more Glamour and less Banality.<br /> <br /> Maybe it should honestly be a shared bar: gaining Banality means losing Glamour, and vice-versa.<br /> <br /> <br /> The other major issue is just the difficulty of recovering Glamour, but this post is long enough as it is.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/827-segev","name":"Segev","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1001"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-21T09:22:52-08:00","id":377269,"title":"","content":"Thinking further, maybe it would be interesting if each Kith had at least one of their traits - the strange obsessive behaviors they have, like Pooka lying and Redcap blood-seeking - be given an active way to express it that generates Glamour. Pooka who get somebody to swallow a fib whole might gain one point (or part of one point?) of Glamour; Redcaps who soak their cap in fresh blood might do similarly. Something a little more active than the Nature/Demeanor willpower-recovery mechanics, but in the same vein.<br /> <br /> They could also have an echo-like taboo that they can break fairly easily, but at the cost of some amount of Glamour (maybe fractional). The higher their Banality rating, the lower the cost in Glamour for violating it. Pooka telling the whole truth, Sidhe breaking a promise/oath, Sluaugh sharing secrets without getting bigger ones in return.<br /> <br /> Tying Echoes to Glamour retention, recovery, and loss would probably be a good design choice all around, honestly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/827-segev","name":"Segev","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1001"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-21T18:02:16-08:00","id":377646,"title":"","content":"I have never read anything from DA: Fae. It sounds like I should probably at least check it out before I run the chronicle I am thinking about.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>I game. This is the way.</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8-storyteller","name":"Storyteller","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"428"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-22T10:12:48-08:00","id":378159,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/268433-fixing-changeling?p=377646#post377646\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have never read anything from DA: Fae. It sounds like I should probably at least check it out before I run the chronicle I am thinking about.</div> </div> </div> </div>If it's the one for the younger folks, there's a bit of a balancing act you'll have to navigate. DA:Fae magic is VERY open ended. Think &quot;Mage&quot; moreso than CtD. Though it's probably more about being &quot;poetically appropriate&quot; than &quot;using a paradigm&quot;. Kids may be GREAT at something that wide open, but they may need something a little more structured. (Or you could just have them play Chagelings as opposed to the other two types and keep them away from doing a lot of Unleashing).<br /> <br /> However, the Art/Realm Structure of CtD is a little opaque and requires hard and fast interpretations to make it less confusing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-22T10:51:46-08:00","id":378199,"title":"","content":"I also think the game would benefit by showing that Changelings are not necessarily the only Fae creatures out there, they are just one type. Integrate the various other Fae more strongly into Changeling society and have them have important roles. Demonstrate that in the core book. Then show why the Changeling's position among the Fae - half faerie and half mortal - is important to that society's survival. It should generate more obvious adventures and interactions since only Changelings can interact with the banal world on an ongoing basis.<br /> <br /> Instead, the books seem to indicate that Changelings are the core type of Fae and dominant the Fae government and population. It would be better if other Fae dominated - at least since the Resurgence - or at least made up a huge bulk of Changeling society. That would make Changelings precious and important to the survival of the Fae. Changelings would have the great task of finding new sources of Glamor and awakening them within mortals.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-22T10:54:10-08:00","id":378202,"title":"","content":"I also think the setting would be improved by alternating between the Unseelie and Seelie courts. Keeping the Seelie court always in charge was a mistake.<br /> <br /> Also better if they were also called the Summer and Winter courts as the Celtic names can be distracting when dealing with non-Celtic fae. &quot;Winter&quot; and &quot;Summer&quot; can be universally applied even in talking about Slavic faeires or Arab Jinn or whatever when Unseelie and Seelie seem appropriate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-22T11:30:13-08:00","id":378247,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/268433-fixing-changeling?p=378199#post378199\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I also think the game would benefit by showing that Changelings are not necessarily the only Fae creatures out there, they are just one type. Integrate the various other Fae more strongly into Changeling society and have them have important roles. Demonstrate that in the core book. Then show why the Changeling's position among the Fae - half faerie and half mortal - is important to that society's survival. It should generate more obvious adventures and interactions since only Changelings can interact with the banal world on an ongoing basis.<br /> <br /> Instead, the books seem to indicate that Changelings are the core type of Fae and dominant the Fae government and population. It would be better if other Fae dominated - at least since the Resurgence - or at least made up a huge bulk of Changeling society. That would make Changelings precious and important to the survival of the Fae. Changelings would have the great task of finding new sources of Glamor and awakening them within mortals.</div> </div> </div> </div>What you're arguing for is that there are wider faerie societies in the Dreaming which can't safely interact with the mundane world, and rely on changelings to encourage more Glamour, is that right?<br /> <br /> Which fae types are predominant in fae society in this take? The major non-changeling fae currently presented in Dreaming are the chimerae, the inanimae, and the adhene, none of which I'd figure to be the dominant figures in a changeling-dependent culture. Or is this more in terms of Dark Ages: Fae, with the changelings as agents for the firstborn and inanimae?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-22T18:18:18-08:00","id":378560,"title":"","content":"Personally, I always felt that the Realms system could use a little work. I know that it was intended to show the chaotic nature of changelings' powers, but it did so in a way that seemed entirely too structured. For example, if you see a changeling use a power on a computer, it is safe to assume that the character could also use the same power on a knife or a cloak. If one uses a power on a friend, they may or may not be able to use it on an enemy as well, but if they use it on an enemy, you know they can use it on a friend. I think the Realms could keep a lot of the same flavor if they removed the 5-dot progression, and made it so that a player chose a facet of the Realm with each dot gained. For example, if two changelings both had Actor 2, one of them might only be able to use their powers on people that they felt strongly about one way or another (True Friend and Dire Enemy), while the other might try to avoid forging too many connections with people in order to maintain the effectiveness of their cantrips (Familiar Face and Complete Stranger). This would allow a player to have the levels of the Realms they want to use more easily, and would leave their powers more unpredictable even to other changelings.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Jason Ross Inczauskis</b>, Freelance Writer<br /> Projects: <i>Dark Eras 2</i>, <i>Mummy: The Curse 2e, Book of Lasting Death, DtR The Clades Companion, Pirates of Pugmire,</i> <i>They Came From Beyond the Grave!, </i><i>TC Aeon: Mission Statements</i>, <i>TC In Media Res,</i> <i>DtD Night Horrors: Enemy Action,</i> <i>C20 Anthology of Dreams</i><br /> Masculine pronouns preferred.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/309-white-oak-dragon","name":"White Oak Dragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"941"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-22T19:18:14-08:00","id":378607,"title":"","content":"I would personally go back to the Mage/Ars Magica roots; particularly Ars Magica. Let the general things you can do be more like Ars Magica. Arts are the &quot;verbs&quot; and Realms are the &quot;nouns,&quot; and let the fae flavor come in after the general capability. I think the Arts/Realms system suffers (and makes Bunks annoying on top of it) from taking the Ars Magica model and then mechanically enforcing flavor on it that sometimes works, and sometimes doesn't. You can use a more solid Bunk system and things like Echoes from DAF to get the fae feel into it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-23T05:43:26-08:00","id":378936,"title":"","content":"I admit that, having only ever owned and read the second edition of C:tD, bunks never really made sense to me. There are (possibly due to poor editing) oblique references to &quot;decks,&quot; which I am given to understand refers to a 1e thing where a random &quot;bunk&quot; was pulled from a deck for each Cantrip. Bunks are, as well, basically the &quot;verbal and somatic components&quot; of the &quot;spell&quot; that makes up the Cantrip's activation, I believe. But it took forever for me to realize that this was supposed to represent a stylistic way that the Changeling did something to make his magic happen.<br /> <br /> He reached into the pocket of the shadow of the person he was pickpocketing with Gimmix. He performed a riverdance where the jumps got higher and higher to perform that prodigious leap.<br /> <br /> The problem is, it seems to be really ill-described. It would probably have benefitted to have sample bunks for each Cantrip and each Realm, with ideas for mixing them for specific uses. As-is, it comes off more silly than thematic, at least to first and even second reading.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/827-segev","name":"Segev","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1001"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-24T21:48:50-08:00","id":380733,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/268433-fixing-changeling?p=378202#post378202\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I also think the setting would be improved by alternating between the Unseelie and Seelie courts. Keeping the Seelie court always in charge was a mistake.<br /> <br /> Also better if they were also called the Summer and Winter courts as the Celtic names can be distracting when dealing with non-Celtic fae. &quot;Winter&quot; and &quot;Summer&quot; can be universally applied even in talking about Slavic faeires or Arab Jinn or whatever when Unseelie and Seelie seem appropriate.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> But half the time Seelie/Unseelie were written as cosmological truths, but other times there'd be African courts are different!, and Nunnhei have different cosmoloigcal spirit truths. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-25T00:15:58-08:00","id":380844,"title":"","content":"For me the greatest problems of CtD were and are Glamour economics, nearly everything costs Glamour and you only have a small pool of it that is rather difficult to fill, and the Realms which were poorly structured. Banality could have used clearer and more structured rules, and such clear and strict rules would only be thematically appropriate for Banality, and a bit less ageism the last of which is rather easy to correct.<br /> <br /> Some quick fixes would be to make Glamour Pool dependent upon Permanent Glamour but much larger, a bit like with Wyrd/Glamour division in CtL, and make gathering Glamour a bit easier like the DtF option of exposing a mortal to your true self and picking up Faith from it. Realms should perhaps be more about the closeness of something to the story at large, that is it is easier to affect something that you have strong ties to, your sworn enemy and true friend alike are close and thus the first things you learn to affect while some passer by or other extra only loosely tied to your story is harder to affect. etc. Also strip away the less needed Glamour cost like from most, if not all, Birthrights.<br /> <br /> Note that those are only patchwork solutions not actual solutions which would require a bit more work than 5 minutes of fast thinking.<br /> <br /> As for DA: Fae. I love the game but it is a different game than CtD. Both have their virtues and weaknesses. I love DA:F magic system that though extremely strong and flexible does have it's risks as well. I dislike the Echo system, well I like the idea behind it but not the execution, that mostly has the effect of driving the PC's to exclusive work amongst the Fae, or other supernaturals, instead of interacting with mortals and it drives that point home a bit too effectively. That is to say it's a bit much that each encounter with a mortal you have has a chance to inflict you with a new Echo. I much more liked the idea of Echoes being tied to your power, more powerful you are the more Echoes you have as enforced by the fact that you have an Echo for each Greater Feature. I love the Kiths of CtD so the lack of them is a minus for me, though it is something I could live with if only the Greater Features would be closer to mechanical strength of Birthrights than they currently are, that is to say while DA:F magic is extremely strong the Greater Features are mechanically much weaker than the Birthrights of CtD which is a shame as it leaves them as small side quirks instead of defining features they are in CtD. I do not mind customisable Kiths but if going for that let the Birthrights keep their oomph.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1629-possessed","name":"Possessed","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1629_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1140"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-26T06:35:23-08:00","id":381896,"title":"","content":"The more I think about it, the more I like the idea of Echoes being something you could <i>buy</i> with experience points. Perhaps a consequence of new Arts or Realms, or perhaps something of their own. Honoring an Echo earns you dribs and drabs of Glamour. Breaking or violating one costs you your faeries mein for a bit, and increases your balanity. Add in some system for banality being a double-endged sword that helps with the balance of mortal vs. fae, and this becomes a potent but risky tool no matter how you use it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/827-segev","name":"Segev","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1001"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-26T16:20:06-08:00","id":382414,"title":"","content":"At first, it felt odd to me that White Wolf would put a High Fantasy venue in a series dedicated to Gothic Horror. Then after I read it, I thought it was okay. Banality explained the Dual Nature and the reason why Adult Changelings (aka Grumps) find it harder to maintain their true nature. (It takes a strong will for a Changeling Grump to have the carefree nature of a Childling, thanks to societial taboos.) Yet a couple of things bothered me.<br /> <br /> 1. Dragons - I LOVED dragons and was hoping that they would be a Kith in Dreaming. Unfortunately, WW decided to delegate them to mechanical villains and I hated that. The fact that Dragons are a Kith in Lost (as the Draconic Kith), I latched onto that. I hope that Dragons will be added as a Kith in the 20 Annie version for Dragon lovers everywhere.<br /> <br /> 2. Magic - The cantrips that Changelings could do is so poorly constructed that it has no substance whatsoever, not unlike the more durable abilities in Lost. Reading that cantrips are WAY too easily destroyed makes the Changelings seem laughable. Make the abilities more durable so that Changelings won't be seen as the bitch race of the World of Darkness.(Compared to the other races, this statement's truth is plausible.)<br /> <br /> Plus, I know that there's Houses, but I couldn't find any sourcebook in Dreaming that talked about them, where are they explained? Also, to give it more seriousness I hope Onyx Path can use the Game of Thrones series as an inspiration of Changeling politics.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/891-jackalopevegas","name":"JackalopeVegas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-26T16:56:45-08:00","id":382466,"title":"","content":"There's actually a lot of material on the Houses.<br /> <br /> The problem with the Houses is that they're primarily a Sidhe thing. Very few non-Sidhe are every going to earn a title in a House. That's one of CtD's more minor issues. You have three &quot;big&quot; factions in the Seelie, Unseelie, and Shadow Court... and then 13 Houses that are almost exclusive to one Kith for the only other major source of political factions. If you try to use the Houses for Changeling Game of Thrones, you end up with the vast majority of focus on the Sidhe since they're the only ones that'll end up mattering.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-26T22:40:10-08:00","id":382758,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JackalopeVegas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/268433-fixing-changeling?p=382414#post382414\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Plus, I know that there's Houses, but I couldn't find any sourcebook in Dreaming that talked about them, where are they explained? Also, to give it more seriousness I hope Onyx Path can use the Game of Thrones series as an inspiration of Changeling politics.</div> </div> </div> </div>There's the Book of Houses series (Book 1 spotlights the five Seelie Houses in the core, Book 2 the three Unseelie Houses of the Shadow Court, and Book 3 the five Houses that hadn't been covered).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-27T07:00:04-08:00","id":383038,"title":"","content":"I always treated it as the commoners being largely sworn to one House or another, much as commoners are in, say, Game of Thrones. They may not hold title, but they live in a, say, House Fiona Freehold, and they are sworn to a House Fiona liege-lord, and they thus view other Houses through that lens.<br /> <br /> Mechanically, the Houses don't do anything for non-sidhe, but that doesn't mean choice of to whom one swears isn't relevant.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/827-segev","name":"Segev","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1001"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-27T10:39:33-08:00","id":383328,"title":"","content":"That's part of the problem with the Houses and so much focus on them.<br /> <br /> The Commoner Kiths have been dealing without the Sidhe at their feudalistic ways for centuries. Most of lived multiple lifetimes and reincarnated into new human hosts with a commensurately more modern concept of how commoners relate to their leaders. The Sidhe return is relatively recent and while they were capable of re-establishing their place as the kings and queens of the fae, there is supposed to be a tension between the commoners that know they don't need the Sidhe, and the Sidhe that want the power they believe they rightfully deserve.<br /> <br /> In a setting were the commoners have mostly sworn to a House, it means the commoners have effectively given up on centuries of their own ways of doing things and capitulated completely to the Sidhe.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-27T11:12:54-08:00","id":383365,"title":"","content":"I tend to assume commoners who are in sidhe-ruled freeholds signed on with Houses because failure to do so meant you had no protection when other sidhe came around to bully you. And made it hard to justify staying in a freehold belonging to a House. The commoner freeholds are, however, far more common, and may or may not swear to a House (but at least pay some lip service to acknowledging sidhe hobility and right to rule, and hope none of them decide to take over their particular home.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/827-segev","name":"Segev","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1001"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-27T23:25:38-08:00","id":384014,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/268433-fixing-changeling?p=383328#post383328\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's part of the problem with the Houses and so much focus on them.<br /> <br /> The Commoner Kiths have been dealing without the Sidhe at their feudalistic ways for centuries. Most of lived multiple lifetimes and reincarnated into new human hosts with a commensurately more modern concept of how commoners relate to their leaders. The Sidhe return is relatively recent and while they were capable of re-establishing their place as the kings and queens of the fae, there is supposed to be a tension between the commoners that know they don't need the Sidhe, and the Sidhe that want the power they believe they rightfully deserve.<br /> <br /> In a setting were the commoners have mostly sworn to a House, it means the commoners have effectively given up on centuries of their own ways of doing things and capitulated completely to the Sidhe.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is not the reading I got.My impression is the Sidhe didn't show up see a Dream republic(or various modern style nation states) spread through out the world and were &quot;Nope time for Feudalism!&quot;. It seemed the majority of Freeholds held to Feudalistic setup. Some even claimed descent from Houses. So the Sidhe show up and suddenly alot of those ratbastard common nobles get kicked out because its like Aragon in Return of the King. Or Arthur returning.<br /> <br /> And then they find out that &quot;the chosen rulers&quot; sometimes are ratbastards too and there is a war, but that wasn't the whole of it.<br /> <br /> <br /> So that leaves Commoners with tension with the Sidhe, the feel right. They look right. They have all the thing a noble should be. Is that what the Dreaming wants? Is that what Changeling society should be? Is it even possible to modernize without letting Banality in? Is acceptance of basic rights of Personhood something good that's come of Banality?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-28T09:28:26-08:00","id":384278,"title":"","content":"It's just as plausible to read it exactly the opposite way. The Sidhe were the complete assholes who LEFT US BEHIND TO DIE while they trotted off to the Dreaming to save their pretty asses. And we've been getting by without them for centuries now, thank you very much. So, if the world was so hard and cold you had to leave it behind, guess what? It's WORSE now, so run along home pretty-pretty....","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-28T11:12:44-08:00","id":384347,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/268433-fixing-changeling?p=384278#post384278\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's just as plausible to read it exactly the opposite way. The Sidhe were the complete assholes who LEFT US BEHIND TO DIE while they trotted off to the Dreaming to save their pretty asses. And we've been getting by without them for centuries now, thank you very much. So, if the world was so hard and cold you had to leave it behind, guess what? It's WORSE now, so run along home pretty-pretty....</div> </div> </div> </div>There's a reason why the return of the Sidhe is called the Ressurgence, It represents a Blossoming of Glamour in the face of previous decay. The sidhe didn't just show up and take Commoner freeholds, they discovered many that were lost, along with treasures unknown before.. And I'll say again it wasn't like Freeholds before the SIdhe came were beacons of equality, they were equally run by despotic nobles or the occasional &quot;Steward&quot;<br /> <br /> That's before the implication that the Sidhe are in fact backed up by the Dreaming to Rule. Can you overthrow them without giving into Banality?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-28T14:38:25-08:00","id":384514,"title":"","content":"Neither of this are universally true or false.<br /> <br /> I'd highly advise reading Fool's Luck; which talks a lot about the Commoner reactions to the Resurgence. Lots of Commoners are split between their feelings of betrayal at both their abandonment by the Sidhe for centuries, and their atrocious actions taken during the Resurgence beyond the pale of simply being Dreaming empowered snobs, and their inherent reaction to the Sidhe's Dreaming empowered right to rule.<br /> <br /> The Sidhe didn't violently steal Freeholds because you can't. But that didn't stop them from persecuting Commoners horribly for not handing over the keys politely. Yes, the Commoner Freeholds weren't bastions of progressive politics, the vast majority left the old school feudalism behind in favor of things closer to modern constitutional monarchies ruled far more by their oligarchic councils than their figure head kings/queens. Modern political thoughts are something that explicitly have started to work their ways into the Commoner Kith mentalities as they've had to live amongst humans for so long.<br /> <br /> There's a reason why the Resurgence lead to a war. Because the Commoners didn't just kneel down and accept the Sidhe as a whole. The Commoners, after the war they lost, might not see value in trying to overthrow the Sidhe now, but that doesn't mean there isn't a deep resentment amongst many of them for how the Sidhe Resurgence turned out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-29T00:42:27-08:00","id":384849,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/268433-fixing-changeling?p=384514#post384514\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Neither of this are universally true or false.<br /> <br /> I'd highly advise reading Fool's Luck; which talks a lot about the Commoner reactions to the Resurgence. Lots of Commoners are split between their feelings of betrayal at both their abandonment by the Sidhe for centuries, and their atrocious actions taken during the Resurgence beyond the pale of simply being Dreaming empowered snobs, and their inherent reaction to the Sidhe's Dreaming empowered right to rule.<br /> <br /> The Sidhe didn't violently steal Freeholds because you can't. But that didn't stop them from persecuting Commoners horribly for not handing over the keys politely. Yes, the Commoner Freeholds weren't bastions of progressive politics, the vast majority left the old school feudalism behind in favor of things closer to modern constitutional monarchies ruled far more by their oligarchic councils than their figure head kings/queens. Modern political thoughts are something that explicitly have started to work their ways into the Commoner Kith mentalities as they've had to live amongst humans for so long.<br /> <br /> There's a reason why the Resurgence lead to a war. Because the Commoners didn't just kneel down and accept the Sidhe as a whole. The Commoners, after the war they lost, might not see value in trying to overthrow the Sidhe now, but that doesn't mean there isn't a deep resentment amongst many of them for how the Sidhe Resurgence turned out.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Every WW book has its biases. Fools Luck has its. The whole &quot;We got along just fine without the Sidhe&quot; easily compares to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ExWfh6sGyso\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ExWfh6sGyso</a> if you ignore Ressurgence of Glamour the rediscovery of countless Freeholds and Treasures the fact that the Dreaming seems more flush and alive than it was for Centuries? <br /> <br /> <br /> This doesn't mean the SIdhe are Good people. This doesn't mean they aren't outdated tyrants built on dreams best forgotten. But that's a question one has to ask is the Ressurgence worth it? Should Kithain be living up to ancient titles because the Dreaming flows through them? Does every attrocity a SIdhe noble commit justify Banality? Are the Sidhe just a representation of how horrible the Dream is how much more comfortable the world might be without it?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-29T09:25:26-08:00","id":385165,"title":"","content":"I think you're taking this far beyond the initial point to argue some specific view of the game that seems derived from picking any choosing what books you want to pay attention to.<br /> <br /> My point was that focusing on the Houses as the primary political factions in the game removes the Commoner/Sidhe antagonism that is supposed to be a central conflict of the game; because it forces Commoners to swear to a House to matter.<br /> <br /> Trying to paint the Sidhe Resurgence as an overall good thing - white washing all of the horrible things they did, and ignoring the impact of centuries of assimilation of new human ideals by the Commoners - only proves my point. Focusing on the Houses removes a primary source of conflict of the game because making the game all about the House means all of those questions you asked as supposedly interesting open ended ones all have to default to whichever answer favors the Sidhe being right.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-29T09:50:04-08:00","id":385202,"title":"","content":"Agreed Heavy. I didn't throw up the Fool's Luck viewpoint as deterministic, just as an example of the simplification of taking one axis and ignoring the others. <br /> <br /> If you want a game where the Sidhe returning was almost entirely good, where the liege's and lackeys where the Commoner characters are the squires and chatelaines to the Sidhe character's knights and lords, it will work perfectly fine and has the potential to be a lot of fun, but that's not the setting we're given. It's much more complicated than that. <br /> <br /> Also, it seems like cause and effect might be a little more complex as well. The game originally and strongly makes the case that the Sidhe return BECAUSE of the Resurgence, not that the Resurgence is a consequence of their return. Which means that the Commoners would be benefiting from it much more if the Sidhe had kept to the Dreaming. That is a wellhead of resentment as well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-29T10:10:42-08:00","id":385236,"title":"","content":"If the Houses are the primary political factions the conflict between Commoners and Nobles, Sidhe particularly but this take also involves the Commoner Nobles, just intensifies because most Commoners will see those of their kind who swear to Houses, even if they do so in an attempt to get political influence to weaken the Sidhe, as joining the enemy. Also it does nothing to remove rebel Commoners from building a political power base of their own, again escalating the conflict, by taking control of Free Holds and swaying the people, which is naturally difficult as the Sidhe have considerable natural edges in this latter endeavor, and rising up to stand against the Houses or forming a &quot;house&quot; of their own demanding a voice of their own.<br /> <br /> The Sidhe by the way are right, they are born of dreams of nobility, rule, tyranny, leadership and beauty and so they truly have been born to rule. The Houses just underline this as does the fact that the Sidhe require a higher amount of Fae Realm to affect as well as their inability to be made the fool through magic not to mention their unbearable and commanding beauty. The question you should be asking is about whether or not such qualities and birthrights alone are enough to make a good leader and whether it is better to be lead by those who think it their right or by those who think it as their privilege. There is a valid reason that the Commoners find the Sidhe leadership hard to bow under just like there are valid reasons for them to rebel.<br /> <br /> So to matter, even politically, one does not need to swear to a House it is just easier that way but when doing so you are selling yourself to the Sidhe. A thought that should spark a rebellious fire inside any commoner who yearns for personal power, freedom or just does not wish to bend knee to anyone.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1629-possessed","name":"Possessed","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1629_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1140"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-29T23:38:13-08:00","id":385971,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/268433-fixing-changeling?p=385165#post385165\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Trying to paint the Sidhe Resurgence as an overall good thing - white washing all of the horrible things they did, and ignoring the impact of centuries of assimilation of new human ideals by the Commoners - only proves my point. Focusing on the Houses removes a primary source of conflict of the game because making the game all about the House means all of those questions you asked as supposedly interesting open ended ones all have to default to whichever answer favors the Sidhe being right.</div> </div> </div> </div>I am not saying the Resurgence is a good thing. I am saying its a neutral thing with potentially horrible bits, it just you know might be what the Dreaming needed to survive. That all these attempts at incorporating equality and liberty might be the soothing comforts of banality. That the horror of Changeling is that there is a One True King a chosen one who is in all ways your better and he doesn't care about you. He doesn't care about Peasant number 5 in his glorious story, that you for all your dreaming soul is just an extra in the SIdhe's story, that at best maybe you get to be a sidekick.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-30T00:18:47-08:00","id":386015,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/268433-fixing-changeling?p=385971#post385971\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...with potentially horrible bits...</div> </div> </div> </div>No. With canonical horrible bits. The Night of Iron Knives isn't some &quot;potential&quot; thing. It's a thing that absolutely happened in CtD's backstory. It was a needlessly brutal act undertaken by the Sidhe forces, violating an offer of peaceful negotiations and ensuring that the Resurgence was settled via the Accordance War leading to countless deaths (including the destruction of fae souls not just human bodies).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That all these attempts at incorporating equality and liberty might be the soothing comforts of banality. That the horror of Changeling is that there is a One True King a chosen one who is in all ways your better and he doesn't care about you. He doesn't care about Peasant number 5 in his glorious story, that you for all your dreaming soul is just an extra in the SIdhe's story, that at best maybe you get to be a sidekick. </div> </div> </div>Well first, High King David does care about you, because he's actually a swell guy. Also, he's disappeared anyway.<br /> <br /> And you're still skipping the point of what I'm saying to grandstand about some personal vision of what CtD is supposed to be.<br /> <br /> All of this talk of the horror of this and that is all well and going. But it isn't about the functional use of the Houses as primary political factions in the game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-30T01:32:25-08:00","id":386071,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/268433-fixing-changeling?p=386015#post386015\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> No. With canonical horrible bits. The Night of Iron Knives isn't some &quot;potential&quot; thing. It's a thing that absolutely happened in CtD's backstory. It was a needlessly brutal act undertaken by the Sidhe forces, violating an offer of peaceful negotiations and ensuring that the Resurgence was settled via the Accordance War leading to countless deaths (including the destruction of fae souls not just human bodies).</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> That wasn't the best phrasing. I meant to say The Ressurgence is Grey with Dark aspects. There's a reason why I used that Monty Python Scene. Yes the Romans brought good things but they also were tyrants who filled hills with forests of crosses. I'm just trying to point out that there's stuff they did good and there are some nice Sidhe, its not all rat bastards all the time. I mean there's a reason the majority of Traditionalists are Commoners.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Well first, High King David does care about you, because he's actually a swell guy. Also, he's disappeared anyway. </div> </div> </div>He's one True King. So is Meilge.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And you're still skipping the point of what I'm saying to grandstand about some personal vision of what CtD is supposed to be.<br /> <br /> All of this talk of the horror of this and that is all well and going. But it isn't about the functional use of the Houses as primary political factions in the game. </div> </div> </div>Yes, I got off track. Fool's luck gives one portrayal of changeling Society, Nobles: the SHining Host gives another. That there is sizable popular support for Both sidhe and commoner nobles. If you go with this view with sizeable retinues, a third of the nobles being nonsidhe. This makes the houses more relevant as social structures. Or that is to say simply enough Noble Houses are important socially when you focus in on Nobles, and less so when you focus on pure commoner issues.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-30T09:36:11-08:00","id":386386,"title":"","content":"Which is fine for your own Changeling games insofar as you make clear to players of non-Sidhe characters of their lesser status across the board. Just so they don't get upset if they get &quot;uppity&quot; and are slapped down by NPCs or the Sidhe PCs.<br /> <br /> There's nothing wrong with that game, it just needs to be be telegraphed in advance that if you play a Commoner kith and don't buy a title, you are a lackey or sidekick and if you do, you are a presumptuous social-climber who doesn't know his place (and that place is REAL, because you ARE inherently lesser than any Sidhe).<br /> <br /> I have players who would probably take that on and we'd have a great chronicle, but there's no way I would spring on it them in play or just assume they get that from reading the books.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-30T10:05:51-08:00","id":386429,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> if you play a Commoner kith and don't buy a title, you are a lackey or sidekick </div> </div> </div>It doesn't mean that you are a sidekick but it should be obvious from the term Commoner that if you do not buy a title you will be politically lesser than Nobles. It's exactly the same if you play any DA chronicle and a player chooses to make a peasant character that player should not be surprised if a noble treats him poorly or that his commands and tries at leadership are ignored in favor of someone with a title, especially if talking about politics.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1629-possessed","name":"Possessed","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1629_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1140"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-30T11:22:54-08:00","id":386513,"title":"","content":"The problem is that the DA playable splats all clearly address how to address this. Human social standing generally doesn't matter, and their own political factions are generally not defined solely by the noble/commoner divide. High Clan vampires and low Clan vampires belong to the same primary political organizations against others, rather than dividing themselves along that line. The Silver Fangs have always had to balance the Garou idea of leadership being something that needs to be earned and defended with their position as the Alpha Tribe; if the Silver Fangs tried to act like the Sidhe and make their Houses the primary political units of DA werewolf society, they'd just get killed for it. Mages really don't give a shit about this. The Inquisition makes everyone equal regardless of who they were born to as they now serve the Church. Etc.<br /> <br /> While DA characters might have to play parts in front of normal people, political power in the DA splats are not simply noble &gt; commoner.<br /> <br /> If you make the Sidhe House the primary political units of the game, you're inherently making it Sidhe &gt; titled Commoners &gt; untitled Commoners in a fashion the game does not present as the default.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-31T11:10:11-08:00","id":387493,"title":"","content":"I agree with Heavy and re-iterate that what you are doing sounds perfectly fine as long as everyone playing in your game knows about it before or at character creation, as this is NOT the primary axis of play as identified in the core rules or in much of the supplemental material. <br /> <br /> However, I don't think that this is the only way to play CtD or the &quot;right&quot; way as put forth in the crunch and fluff (not that that matters a fig). There are plenty of other options that equally supported by the material and a lot that are even more holistic or more strongly supported. This way is taking one axis of a potential chronicle and minimizing many others. In fact, it is pretty much taking that one axis and ignoring fully half of it. <br /> <br /> That being said, those are often the kind of choices made when setting up and running a particular chronicle. I don't really see a problem running a chronicle the way you are putting out there and would probably enjoy playing in it. In fact, if I wanted to focus on some of the core metaplot elements, I might even run some version of it. I do not, however, see this as the &quot;default&quot; setting for the game. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-31T21:45:49-08:00","id":388003,"title":"","content":"I just had a thought, what if Houses were made more like NWOD Y splats?(Covenants, Orders, Tribes etc) with them broadly becoming the basic social networks of changeling society?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-01T02:07:59-08:00","id":388152,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure it works well in that set-up. CtD already has Kith and Seemings to cover the basic X and Y axis stuff. If you're going to introduce the Houses in that paradigm, it would be as a Z-splat. Something optional with extra perks and duties and such that some characters will do and others don't.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-01T02:26:21-08:00","id":388178,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> it would be as a Z-splat. Something optional with extra perks and duties and such that some characters will do and others don't. </div> </div> </div>This sounds rather apt description of Houses as they currently are. Those interested in Noble affairs, perks and duties, will do them others don't.<br /> <br /> The reason I say this is that I have run plenty of CtD Chronicles in my time, and a CtD LARP as well, and the issue you are describing has never come up. Rather it has always been like I said, the Houses act as a further wedge between the Commoners and the Sidhe/Nobles driving the conflict on as without them it would wither away.<br /> <br /> The thing is if a Commoners without a Title are a meaningful political force as is then the division between Noble and Commoner starts to loose it's meaning, outside of mechanical differences of course, if on the other hand Nobles are the ones holding Political power and they divide themselves to Houses, so as to not be a monolithic block, the Commoners have a reason to fight for their rights and there is actual potential for conflict ever present there. As well as providing the tasty conflict of Commoner Nobles vs Commoners the latter will think the former as sell outs for the most part while the former will think of their entitlement as a necessary part of wielding political might, some telling themselves that they fight the good fight from the inside while others truly have sold out their Commoner kin.<br /> <br /> But this is only my experience on the matter.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1629-possessed","name":"Possessed","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1629_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1140"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-02-01T12:52:45-08:00","id":388774,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Possessed</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/268433-fixing-changeling?p=388178#post388178\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This sounds rather apt description of Houses as they currently are. Those interested in Noble affairs, perks and duties, will do them others don't.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, sort of. Being a Sidhe without a House is very different from being another Kith without one. The Houses ability to act as a Z-splat in the nWOD paradigm is also hampered by how the X- and Y-splats of CtD are handled; which is less of an issue of the Houses as written then other things.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...and the issue you are describing has never come up. </div> </div> </div>Because from the sound of it you're running the Houses as written, and the relationship between Commoners and Nobles as written, rather than increasing the importance of Houses in the game; which is what Ajax and I are responding to.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The thing is if a Commoners without a Title are a meaningful political force as is then the division between Noble and Commoner starts to loose it's meaning, outside of mechanical differences of course, if on the other hand Nobles are the ones holding Political power and they divide themselves to Houses, so as to not be a monolithic block, the Commoners have a reason to fight for their rights and there is actual potential for conflict ever present there. </div> </div> </div>I fail to see how that's the case. It isn't mutually exclusive with the Commoners that reject/can't get a title vs. Commoners that join up conflict either.<br /> <br /> The Commoners without a Title are a meaningful political force because they are the most populous type of changeling and have been around the longest. The Accordance War is what knocked them down enough to give space to the other political forces in the current state of the game. The Houses certainly are important for keeping the Noble power base from simply overwhelming the Commoners with out a Title political force. The Commoners have plenty of reasons to hate the Nobles without the Houses. The Houses keep the Nobles fractured and in-fighting - which gives the anti-Noble commoners space - but it doesn't generate impetus for anti-Noble political force on its own.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-27T06:57:28-07:00","id":529890,"title":"","content":"I just want to echo the sentiment that a &quot;fixed&quot; CtD is basically DA:Fae. It works well in modern times too and there is some good stuff for inspiration. I especially like the books Tithe/Ironsides/Valiant for modern Fae inspiration.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3075-coridan","name":"Coridan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3075_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"528"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-27T08:28:26-07:00","id":529992,"title":"","content":"DA: Fae is a great game, but it and Changeling: The Dreaming address different themes. Changeling: The Dreaming is also a great game. One isn't a &quot;fixed&quot; version of the other. They are two separate games with different design goals. There's no reason the two games can't coexist.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Charlie Cantrell</b><br /> Onyx Path Freelancer <hr />Changeling: The Dreaming 20th Anniversary Edition, Conquering Heroes, Book of Freeholds, Guide to the Night, C20 Players Guide","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/648-pookaknight","name":"PookaKnight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar648_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"690"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-27T10:15:02-07:00","id":530098,"title":"","content":"I mean mechanically. I would much prefer to see CtD adopt the Dominions/Unleashing/Cantrip system and features system. The kith splats were just silly, making entire splats for every single Fae concept ever.<br /> <br /> Theme wise I would absolutely prefer the glamour/banality concepts in a modern Fae game rather than the Oaths. A good CtD20 would be a mesh of the two, since CtD needed a 'Revised' anyway it shouldn't just be a reprint of 2e poor design.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3075-coridan","name":"Coridan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3075_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"528"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-27T18:05:50-07:00","id":530463,"title":"","content":"Though CtD is the only RPG I still play, I'm completely aware of its problems and incoherences. As a storyteller, along these almost 20 years, I could point out what bothered and/or confused more my players:<br /> 1. What's the main conflict of the game? I mean, is it about a dying race living and drowning in a noxious world that infects and deteriorates its own very nature as the years go by? Or is it about people that refuses to lose their innocence so they keep childish for as much as they can? It can't be both.<br /> 2. More identification with fairy tales. The main kiths are ok, but as the line has developed, more narrower and specific kiths started to appear, it was a chaos! Nunnehi, menehune, hsien, dark kin, inanimae, chimeras, etc, they all try to explain mythological creatures but actually none generated a trully identification with any fairy tale. Sometimes it's ok to be not very original.<br /> 3. Cantrips. Realms are just so limiting, arts are just so incoherent (specially Primal, Legerdemain and Chicanery). And WHY changelings can't cast illusions as Ravnos' Chemistry ou deal with dreams as Wraith's Phantasm? All I see in the Core Rulebook is how Changelings deal in a daily basis with dreams, but they don't have anything (in rules terms) to deal with it actually. On the other hand, the whole story tells about fairies hiding behind illusions (and it is also highlighted in other WoD books), but the truth is that changelings can create just dull illusions.<br /> 4. What's the point to put technology = banality and anachronism = glamour? It's not only the Nockers that can be inspired by technology.<br /> 5. Chimerical x Real. It must be more simple. Players, in general, get tired of asking what they can or cannot see/do along the game session.<br /> <br /> There's another things that bother me and the dozens of players that I played with, but I think these points are constants.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5821-neon-sidhe","name":"Neon.Sidhe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5821_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"65"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-28T00:48:11-07:00","id":530732,"title":"","content":"I just want to go on record and say that I love Changeling the dreaming. somethings could use some work though...<br /> <br /> Banality: I never cared for the science=faerie death mentality that the first and second editions seem hellbent on hammering home. that's not even mentioning the game actually expects me to believe that standing in line at the post office is going to kill you. Personally i think it should be more along the lines of a broken heart freezes the soul and makes dreams die kind of thing.<br /> <br /> More clairty on how chimeria work. How does it (not) interact with changelings and the rest of the world.<br /> <br /> Bedlam given an actual system.<br /> <br /> a more universal system for the various kiths. I get that thallian, adhene, nuhennie and merfolk and kithian are all different, but why do they all need a completely different mechanics?<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5767-deedee","name":"DeeDee","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5767_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"116"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-28T14:08:10-07:00","id":531249,"title":"","content":"Changeling had its failures, but I think it was a great game.<br /> I don't understand why some of you want that Changeling20 becomes DA: FAE 20. Yeah, there was some great ideas in DA: FAE, but right now I want some Changeling love. I wanna see some of its failures fixed, but I don't want to get the book and find a game I can't recognize.<br /> For me the big Changeling problem was it was too easy that a Changeling game becomes some kind of cheesy hiper-fantasy adventure. I miss some darker core conflicts. I know the game had them, but most of the time they were completely pass over most of the time.<br /> This don't wanna say there is no work to do about Cantrips, I like the system but it need some little fixings, or Kiths. I like Kiths splash, but some of them needs some revision. I don't think we need an universal system for all changeling families. I like different cultures having different dreams and creating different faes, I totally understand some different mechanics for all of them.<br /> <br /> So, for me the problem is that Changeling didn't use the dark / sad / painful parts of its own universe more than previous editions. If not, Changeling probably will keep being the cartoonish brother of World of Darkness.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/n6g3njp\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/kpa5GS5Mp\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://i.imgsafe.org/93820aaad7.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/584-eliseo-ap-liam","name":"Eliseo ap Liam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar584_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"18"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-28T17:11:26-07:00","id":531403,"title":"","content":"I always had an issue with Banality in Changeling as the force of human disbelief, and setting it up as the dichotomous opposite of creativity and imagination. It kind of represents a &quot;liberal arts&quot; understanding of science and mathematics to suggest that these disciplines somehow undermine any sense of wonder and joy with which we view the universe. This is <a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/MeasuringTheMarigolds\" target=\"_blank\">Measuring the Marigolds</a> mentality, and comes across as hostile to those that find joy in biology or love the explanatory power of mathematics. We see much the same thing in early editions of Mage in which the bad guys are the force behind the scientific paradigm that gave us indoor plumbing, antibiotics and Taco Bell.<br /> <br /> My objection to a fundamental premise of the game suggests that my solution for &quot;fixing&quot; it involves scrapping the entire thing and building up from scratch. This problem is not present in Changeling; the Lost, by the way, which is why I tend to prefer that game to Dreaming.<br /> <br /> <img src=\"http://i.gyazo.com/a40355d793f3b3f4559b38a301326c6f.png\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I seem to have acquired a site for running play by post games. This is unexpected and frightening and come watch either the glorious play or the magnificent train wreck:<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://madnessforums.com/forums/index.php\" target=\"_blank\">The Malkavian Madness Network</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1515-benjcano","name":"BenjCano","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1515_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1276"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-29T06:34:50-07:00","id":531921,"title":"","content":"Would it be possible to make Banality a sort of &quot;balancing stat&quot; for Changelings, If they have none they risk to dissapear like the dreams they are, if they have too much they risk to forget they are not humans?<br /> <br /> Would that counts as &quot;fixing&quot; at least a bag of issues with Dreaming?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2225-ganonso","name":"ganonso","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2225_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-29T07:11:07-07:00","id":531944,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ganonso</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/268433-fixing-changeling?p=531921#post531921\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Would it be possible to make Banality a sort of &quot;balancing stat&quot; for Changelings, If they have none they risk to dissapear like the dreams they are, if they have too much they risk to forget they are not humans?<br /> <br /> Would that counts as &quot;fixing&quot; at least a bag of issues with Dreaming?</div> </div> </div> </div>At that point, you're turning Banality into Changeling: The Lost's Clarity. The primary theme of Changeling: The Dreaming isn't holding on to your sanity. That's Lost's territory. Changeling: The Dreaming is about facing the death of dreams and wonder - the slow erosion of your vital spark.<br /> <br /> Changeling's biggest problem with how it presented Banality was inconsistency. It shows in this thread. It all depends on which sections of which books you read to come away thinking that science somehow equals Banality. In other places, the core book stresses there is nothing inherently banal about science. That's certainly the take I run with, and the game's backstory backs that up.<br /> <br /> Woodstock and Apollo 11's moon landing happened less than a month apart. Woodstock, despite its place in popular culture, is rarely talked about in Changeling. Apollo 11, one of the greatest scientific and engineering achievements of the 20th century, is explicitly the catalyst causing such a massive upsurge in glamour in the world that even the gates to Arcadia opened briefly once more.<br /> <br /> This isn't to say science can't be banal, but so can painting or music composition. It's all about the approach taken not the activity itself.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Charlie Cantrell</b><br /> Onyx Path Freelancer <hr />Changeling: The Dreaming 20th Anniversary Edition, Conquering Heroes, Book of Freeholds, Guide to the Night, C20 Players Guide","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/648-pookaknight","name":"PookaKnight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar648_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"690"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-29T08:24:56-07:00","id":531968,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PookaKnight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/268433-fixing-changeling?p=531944#post531944\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It all depends on which sections of which books you read to come away thinking that science somehow equals Banality</div> </div> </div> </div>I would be more open to this argument, that science was not necessarily banal, if not for the specific description of Banality as &quot;The curtain of doubt and rationality that humans raised centuries ago to explain away their fears not only separates the mortal world from the Dreaming, it also erodes the spark of creativity that gives rise to hope and imagination.&quot;<br /> <br /> Which is just insulting to say that humans came up with the scientific method to explain away their fears, instead of because it's the most consistent and reliable way to acquire knowledge about the world in which we live in, and that it's beneficial to a person to believe as many true things and as few false things as possible.<br /> <br /> When a high school guidance counselor or a parent tells a child not to bank on becoming a professional dancer or NBA player, this is presented as banal by Changeling, and the counselor or parent is portrayed as the bad guy killing the human spirit. Instead of, you know, trying to adequately their student or their child that they have their heart set on a field in which very few people succeed.<br /> <br /> I take serious issue with any game system that portrays blind faith as a good thing, and tries to tell me that doubt and rationality and realism are bad.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I seem to have acquired a site for running play by post games. This is unexpected and frightening and come watch either the glorious play or the magnificent train wreck:<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://madnessforums.com/forums/index.php\" target=\"_blank\">The Malkavian Madness Network</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1515-benjcano","name":"BenjCano","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1515_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1276"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-29T09:09:34-07:00","id":531995,"title":"","content":"Yep, that's one of the lines that say science is banal. There are lots of others that say science isn't banal. It's that whole inconsistency thing I was talking about.<br /> <br /> Which is the true intention for Changeling? Like I said, I think Changeling's backstory is clear on the matter. Also telling is that mechanically, there are no banality gains for practicing the scientific method. Plus, there's a whole kith based around research and invention.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Charlie Cantrell</b><br /> Onyx Path Freelancer <hr />Changeling: The Dreaming 20th Anniversary Edition, Conquering Heroes, Book of Freeholds, Guide to the Night, C20 Players Guide","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/648-pookaknight","name":"PookaKnight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar648_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"690"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-29T14:43:16-07:00","id":532304,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BenjCano</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/268433-fixing-changeling?p=531968#post531968\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> When a high school guidance counselor or a parent tells a child not to bank on becoming a professional dancer or NBA player, this is presented as banal by Changeling, and the counselor or parent is portrayed as the bad guy killing the human spirit. Instead of, you know, trying to adequately their student or their child that they have their heart set on a field in which very few people succeed.<br /> <br /> I take serious issue with any game system that portrays blind faith as a good thing, and tries to tell me that doubt and rationality and realism are bad.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think this is an example of EXACTLY what Banality is and should be in CtD. Not the &quot;Banal = Science-y stuff 'cause it's cold and hard&quot; Taking someones dreams (NOTE: <i>dreams, </i>as in &quot;Dreaming&quot;) and stomping on them because they have very little chance to succeed is definitely grabbing their heart in a cold fist of iron and crushing it. <br /> <br /> I also don't thing that RPG's are the best place to get guidance counseling on preparing for future success vis-a-vis the hardships of living in the real actual world. For a lot of players, Hell, I'd go so far as to say for most players and ST's gaming is their escape from such things. In fact, Changeling my capture that implicit idea more explicitly than any other game, now that I think about it. (Wow... I can't believe I said that...) <br /> <br /> Designing and playing RPGs would be rich with Glamour and would very rarely be Banal. Even something like old old Traveller's &quot;Billion Credit Squadron&quot;, which is essentially a supplement about <i>budgeting,</i> was rich with creative juices. Even the minutia of running a gaming company with all the folderol that goes along with that was all about being creative... even editing. <br /> <br /> I guess it might be unfortunate if an impressionable person played Changeling and decided the universe was more to their taste as a place where they didn't need to use rational postivism to grapple with its situations and vicissitudes. But, then again, it might not be. Maybe they'll do what most artistic people do when they follow those dreams and find some way to make the essentials work why they do something completely impractical with their lives. Then again, most people aren't using the scientific method to make life choices in any case.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-29T17:01:45-07:00","id":532580,"title":"","content":"To me Banality is stuff like falling into a rut. The best example of Banality is this <a href=\"https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=K38xNqZvBJI\" target=\"_blank\">https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=K38xNqZvBJI</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3075-coridan","name":"Coridan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3075_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"528"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-02T10:22:06-07:00","id":537190,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PookaKnight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/268433-fixing-changeling?p=531944#post531944\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">At that point, you're turning Banality into Changeling: The Lost's Clarity. The primary theme of Changeling: The Dreaming isn't holding on to your sanity. That's Lost's territory. Changeling: The Dreaming is about facing the death of dreams and wonder - the slow erosion of your vital spark.</div> </div> </div> </div>true, you have a valid point. but balancing the dreaming with the mortal world is also a theme in The Dreaming that kind of got pushed aside in previous additions. the lack of a definant system for Bedlam was a big part of that. one of the things that always interested me about changeling was the way it approached magic. Use to much and you loose your mind, use too little and you lose your soul. its kind of like Harnano in Werewolf, its supposed to be there and be a pit fall for any player character to fall into, but it somehow never really pops up in the system or characters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5767-deedee","name":"DeeDee","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5767_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"116"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-03T18:58:43-07:00","id":539040,"title":"","content":"One thing that always bugged me was the association of Banality with &quot;growing up.&quot; Sure I get where it's coming from, but it needs to be developed more. The burdens of adulthood absolutely have the potential to crush someone's spirit, but holding down a job shouldn't be inherently banal. Living in the basement, smoking pot and playing video games all day carries just as much danger of Banality as working in a cubicle. To me Banality represents the point at which something has stopped growing, stopped being dynamic, and has started decaying. Banality is defeat and stagnation. <br /> <br /> With that in mind I would propose reworking how the Seemings operate. Decouple them somewhat from a hard chronological age and link them to one's ability to regain Glamour and withstand the press of Banality. <br /> <br /> As a Childling everything carries with it a certain sense of wonder, Glamour is easy to find but you find yourself easily hurt and frustrated by the cold realities of life, making you vulnerable to Banality. <br /> <br /> As a Wilder you're trapped in a half-step. You've tasted bitterness and compromise but the struggle is what you live for. Glamour takes a little more work, but that just makes it sweeter. Banality still stings, but what doesn't kill you only makes your stranger.<br /> <br /> Becoming a Grump is hard. Some have even likened it to a second Chrysalis. Those that survive though are stronger for it. You've learned how to balance the responsibilities of your &quot;normal&quot; life and your Changeling life. You've accepted what you cannot change and your skin is thicker against the ravages of Banality. Unfortunately this cynicism often makes it harder to feel the pure rush of the Dreaming, and Glamour is more difficult to come by.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-03T20:19:37-07:00","id":539124,"title":"","content":"The Pathfinder default setting has something called the Bleaching for Gnomes which is very reminiscent of Banality <a href=\"http://pathfinderwiki.com/wiki/Bleaching\" target=\"_blank\">http://pathfinderwiki.com/wiki/Bleaching</a>. Gnomes basically have to adventure/experiment/try new things to stave it off lest they become depressed and insane.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3075-coridan","name":"Coridan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3075_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"528"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-08T16:22:34-07:00","id":545957,"title":"","content":"I'm not keen on the suggestions to 'fix' the game which ultimately boil down to 'turn it into DA:F20' or 'make it CtL'. If the game were vastly overhauled, I don't think it would be in the *20 spirit. V20, W20 and M20 are all 'love letters' to their fans. Making sweeping changes to CtD that would make it another game wouldn't be honouring its fans, but instead trying to please people who didn't like the game in the first place. So I really wouldn't buy C20 if it was a vastly different game. I currently ST one game of CtD and play in another, and while it needed a Revised Edition, it wasn't as hugely broken as some make out. It requires house rules and a few tweaks, but it does work.<br /> <br /> I like that you can play urban fantasy of the sort that Charles De Lint writes, but also steampunk adventure and 'D&amp;D in the City', if that's what you really want. It's great for the kind of metro-mythic setting of China Mieville's King Rat, Jeff Noon's Vurt (if the trips were journeys into the Dreaming and the posthuman stuff were merely chimerical reality), Pan's Labyrinth, and Gaiman's Neverwhere and The Sandman.<br /> <br /> That said, there are some recurring issues that should be addressed:<br /> <br /> 1. Banality should be dealt with more consistently, and in a more nuanced way. It needs to be more than 'science' or 'mundanity'. We also need some explanation as to its exact nature if, as with the Dauntain, it actually seems to be a kind of 'dark magic' as well as the crushing weight of shattered dreams. It's not Paradox. Perhaps it's just another kind of magic--a paradigm antithetical to that of the Dreaming. That would be more interesting, because it allows for Banality to be both of the facets we've seen so far. But then, we need to learn more about its origins and limitations.<br /> <br /> 2. Linked to the above, we probably should de-couple age and Banality. The Seemings should be more metaphysical phases than fixed age ranges. It doesn't make sense that someone hits 25 and then suddenly becomes a Grump. But an 80-year old, hardened changeling who's battled Banality and been scarred by it? Yeah, okay.<br /> <br /> 3. Realms can be limiting, although I like the concept of them. There are a few solutions. You could provide more dots for starting characters (even starting with 7 instead of 5 would make a difference--although 10 would be too many). But I think it's actually the Arts that are a bigger problem. It's the combination of a limited power (Art) and a further limiting Realm that makes cantrips too narrow. The system is otherwise not as difficult as it seems. I'd probably broaden the powers more, or make them cheaper and offer more alternate level powers. An even simpler option would be to bring in an optional rule to have 'Arts' as freeform powers limited only by Realms. They'd be like Spheres, but with much narrower focus (Wayfare is travel, Sovereign is command, Primal is enhancing the body with nature). You'd have to bump up their cost, though, or maybe require Glamour for all cantrips.<br /> <br /> 4. In my own experience, Glamour's about as easy to regain as Pathos. It can be more or less scarce depending on how much dross the ST brings into the game and how many freeholds there are in a city. Unlike other critters, changelings can cast chimerical cantrips without spending any Glamour at all, if they want. This means you can get away with casting lots of cantrips in all-fae company without spending any Glamour at all. I very rarely spend Glamour when playing a changeling character. But making it easier isn't a bad idea, either. It allows for characters without easy access to a freehold to regain Glamour much more easily. I'd just let them draw directly from people's dreams like in the BFG.<br /> <br /> 5. Mood. I expect vast changes would just see something similar to the Edition Wars that plagued Mage. But showing the darker side of the setting wouldn't go amiss. The fluff and artwork didn't help, since everything featured dragons and bunny rabbits and balloons. There's no reason why chimerae wouldn't take more urban forms--giant king rats, concrete golems, dragons made of car parts. There's no reason why the pervasive ambience of the World of Darkness shouldn't be expressed even in the Near Dreaming as whirling, delirious storms and shuddering swarms of chittering flies. It's always made sense to me. Dreams aren't always candyfloss and unicorns; they come from the id and are full of perversion, repression and neurosis.<br /> <br /> Not mentioned before, but also something of an issue, is healing. You need Primal 4 just to heal damage using magic. This means that many players just take the Regeneration Merit, which is conversely quite powerful. Meanwhile, wraiths and vampires just spend a point of Pathos/blood to heal a level of normal damage. I feel like the Holly Strike and Heather Balm powers should be split, putting the healing power at a lower level and keeping the attack as Primal 4. Alternatively, they could just allow changelings to spend Glamour to heal themselves (or at least their fae miens).<br /> <br /> In terms of the 'dual vision' issue some people have had (it's never really been an issue for me), it's always occurred to me that you can go the route Nancy A. Collins uses for her Pretenders in the Sonja Blue series: changelings have to 'shift' their vision into the chimerical spectrum, as it were, but they can do this without spending Glamour. It would bring their supernatural vision in line with the Deathsight/Lifesight powers of wraiths, and prevent things getting too confusing for players who just can't envisage two realities at once (which, if you think about it, would give most changelings a really bad headache). In-game this could be an effect of the Reckoning/the thickening of the Gauntlet/the increased presence of Banality.<br /> <br /> I don't want a radically different CtD for C20. I just want one with better rules and more suggestions for how we can play the game in a variety of ways.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":5291,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","user":"adambeyoncelowe","date":". 06-09-2015, 07:03 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-09T00:50:45-07:00","id":546637,"title":"","content":"I've been considering reworking the realms myself. the descriptions in 2E feel rather arbitrary and random to me. I've been toying with the idea that each dot covers a level of familiarity and intimacy with the phenomenon in question. in regards<br /> <br /> *- intimately familiar you see and interact with the target everyday and know it well. like a loved one you live with, a favored tool, a tree you climb all the time, a mortal enemy or your own magic spells.<br /> **-familiar- you see and interact on a fairly regular basis, you know its name but don't know it intimately. a co worker, a good friend, a chimera who lives in your neighborhood.<br /> ***-passer by: you see it often but rarely interact with it beyond the most superficial ways. the guy you occasionally make small talk with in the elevator, the office building that you run errands to on a semi regular basis but don't work in. the fast food place you stop by on occasion.<br /> ****-the familiar stranger- people and things you have some familiarity with but don't really know beyond the most superficial level. the guy you see on your walks but don't know his name, the news anchor you watch every night but never met. the lake you've passed on your way to work but never dipped your feet into. an enemy's loyal henchmen you've punched a few times.<br /> *****site unseen. you've never seen or interacted with this person or phenomenon in any shape or form before.<br /> <br /> thoughts?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5767-deedee","name":"DeeDee","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5767_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"116"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-09T04:05:36-07:00","id":546725,"title":"","content":"DeeDee, those definitions make sense. I'd perhaps tweak the descriptions to give an example for each Realm at each level, and I'd question whether a good friend or an enemy would be more intimately known, but otherwise it works.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-09T11:23:09-07:00","id":547362,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>adambeyoncelowe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/268433-fixing-changeling?p=546725#post546725\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">DeeDee, those definitions make sense. I'd perhaps tweak the descriptions to give an example for each Realm at each level, and I'd question whether a good friend or an enemy would be more intimately known, but otherwise it works.</div> </div> </div> </div>thanks, as for the enemies, I was going by the logic that in The Dreaming, with the practicality of Chimerical damage, the chances of you getting to know your archnemesis on a very personal level is quite high, think along the the lines of Batman and Joker.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5767-deedee","name":"DeeDee","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5767_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"116"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-14T12:29:41-07:00","id":554670,"title":"","content":"Step 1 - Solve the game's Tone problem - Changelings are happy, bright creatures fading away from existence. The book's colorful and happy, but the game's themes are dark as hell. Either pick a direction, or give the game a better focus ON THIS DICHOTOMY. <br /> <br /> Currently, Dreaming mumbles quietly to itself what its' theme should be. Instead, the game's whole book should scream &quot;Do Not Go Gently Into The Quiet of the Night. Rage, Rage, Against the Dying of the Light.&quot;<br /> <br /> Step 2 - banality is the mechanic by which the fae are doomed. If it's a gaeas preventing them from changing, that's on thing, but it would work much better with one change - Instead of things that are tied to the mundanity of the world, tie it to what it should have been to begin with - The Opposite of hope. Banality should be the chains that hold you to the mundane life, that keep you from making dreams reality. IT should NOT be something that penalizes you for using technology to make your dreams happen, or from touching vampires (especially when you're slaying them.) Giving in to despair, being held down by the world around you - these are the things that should give banality, not Virtual Adepts' constructed realms or a trip through the underworld (a magical experience, even if it is depressing). <br /> <br /> Step 3 - Include the dreams of the modern world. Rocket ships, Aliens, superheroes, Hollywood Glamour and Bollywood madness, Cowboys and Indians, Spy games and Heist thrillers. If you're going to feed off dreams, have some dreams that are more common for the common man than Kings and Crowns. <br /> <br /> Step 4 - Cut the giggly cartoon aspect. IT has its' place, and it's a great way to get people to underestimate you. People forget you have the ability to violate physics if you make them laugh. Rewrite the powers to strip out some of the Merry Melodies antics. <br /> <br /> Step 5 -Solve the Chimerical problem - Don't Let Changelings hide in the emperor's new clothes. Changelings that are physically different need to be physically different. your &quot;Seeming&quot; should be a physical change visible to all. A Fae form is the true form, a human shape the banal mask that you hide behind. You can hide mystical artifacts as mundane crap, but doing so locks away their power. By removing the chimerical aspect of these things, you solve the problem of the Troll in the Doorway - In troll form, he ducks. In Mortal form, he's already taken the banality hit. Illusions are one thing. &quot;IT's This, but it's really this, But it's this to humans, But itt's THIS to mages.&quot; is needless complexity. <br /> <br /> Step 6 - Make the Seelie/Unseelie thing something less of a binary and more of a pair of lines you have to cross. This isn't DnD, this is the world of darkness. Why is there no shade of Grey?<br /> <br /> Step 7 - More Alien-ness, more Other-worldlyness. I'm not talking about chimerical garbage, I'm talking about the fact that you're the soul of an N-dimensional being grafted onto a human body and restricted to existing in 4.5 dimensions (you can only move in one direction in Time). <br /> <br /> Step 8 - Find out what happened to the soul you replaced, since you're a changeling. <br /> <br /> Step 9 - Relegate fae courts' current structure to a minority and/or the past. For a game about creatures of dynamism, it's static as hell. Feudalism is for Vampires.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/55-necrotek","name":"necrotek","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar55_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"38"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-23T02:26:44-07:00","id":565131,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>necrotek</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/268433-fixing-changeling?p=554670#post554670\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Step 1 - Solve the game's Tone problem - Changelings are happy, bright creatures fading away from existence. The book's colorful and happy, but the game's themes are dark as hell. Either pick a direction, or give the game a better focus ON THIS DICHOTOMY.<br /> <br /> Currently, Dreaming mumbles quietly to itself what its' theme should be. Instead, the game's whole book should scream &quot;Do Not Go Gently Into The Quiet of the Night. Rage, Rage, Against the Dying of the Light.&quot;<br /> <br /> Step 2 - banality is the mechanic by which the fae are doomed. If it's a gaeas preventing them from changing, that's on thing, but it would work much better with one change - Instead of things that are tied to the mundanity of the world, tie it to what it should have been to begin with - The Opposite of hope. Banality should be the chains that hold you to the mundane life, that keep you from making dreams reality. IT should NOT be something that penalizes you for using technology to make your dreams happen, or from touching vampires (especially when you're slaying them.) Giving in to despair, being held down by the world around you - these are the things that should give banality, not Virtual Adepts' constructed realms or a trip through the underworld (a magical experience, even if it is depressing).</div> </div> </div> </div>^This. Anyone heard of a nWoD fan game called <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/home\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: The Hopeful</a>?<br /> <br /> It's basically New World of Darkness's version of the magical girl genre, that tries to strike a balance between the bubblegum sweetness of Sailor Moon...and the potential for seriousness and emotional trauma of Madoka Magica and Sailor Nothing. From what little I've read from the book itself, Princess: The Hopeful is an attempt to transplant the ideas of Changeling: The Dreaming into nWoD (since Changeling: The Lost is a much different game with its own goals). The player characters use magic more akin to Cantrips, swear allegiance to ancient dream royalty, and they gain power by going out and living (in this case, by interacting with a host of friends and loved ones, like a proper magical girl ought to do).<br /> <br /> But most importantly, it contextualizes their struggle by 1) giving them an actual adversary to fight (the forces of elemental darkness itself), and 2) by making their personal battle about promoting Hope and combating Despair.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-30T14:27:30-07:00","id":574346,"title":"","content":"From a gameplay standpoint, unless you want grumps to just be &quot;worse&quot; at being Changelings than wilders, and wilders to be &quot;worse&quot; than childlings, banality needs to have some attractiveness to it.<br /> <br /> I am not sure that I agree that it cannot represent, to some extent, a rooting in the real world. That doesn't inherently make it &quot;clarity from C:tL.&quot; Keep it as a denial of dreams. To borrow from Gurren Lagann, glamour is &quot;spiral power:&quot; it is hope that simply dreaming and wishing hard enough can make things come true. Banality is realizing that this is not so.<br /> <br /> Glamour is power gained from accepting your narrative role and expanding upon it. From deciding what you want and being willing to believe in it and in your self-image. That self-image includes a manic pixie from before time that follows alien laws; you must adhere to them to fuel and sustain your glamour, because the same rules that say you can't cross a line of salt are the ones that say you can leap across the city in your 7-league boots. Banality destroys your ability to do the impossible...but it also declares that you're not a silly child to be restrained by stupid games. Banality accepts reality and rejects fantasy. It is the mysterious power of mankind to make choices unburdened by fictional limitations...but it also burdens them with mundane reality and inability to create wonders.<br /> <br /> This is a WoD game; &quot;mundane humanity&quot; should not be the goal. It hsould be something worrisome. But there should be power in it, especially compared to the world of the Changelings. Grumps are adults with the pwoer to drive, to go to work and earn money, to buy things. These mysterious grown-up powers are, to children, wondrous and tempting, and the children rarely see the true costs to them. (At the same time, it is a nostalgic filter that makes adults think kids have &quot;the life&quot; and just don't appreciate it; adults really ARE freer, with more choices and opportunities open to them. Responsibility is not, in reality, the burden we tend to think it is.)<br /> <br /> But in the context of this game setting, the grump is clinging to his childhood dreams and striving to harness powers he often reminds himself don't really exist to make things happen.<br /> <br /> The dichotomy of this tale shoudl be both the fading of a glorious and wondrous people into dust, and growing up. One can be a metaphor for the other, while clinging to the notion that that inner spark of childhood wonder is critical to the grown-up being truly successful.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Mechanically, Realms should probably be more permissive than restrictive. Things like Scene and Time, which expand on what you <i>can</i> do, should be the rule, rather than Fae, Actor, Prop, etc., which are restrictive (i.e. &quot;you can't affect changelings without Fae&quot;). It could be as simple as changing them to die-pool elements, rather than &quot;you must have this rank to effect this thing.&quot; Still, there's poetry to the way they were laid out...it just made for bad game mechanics. If the meanings of the ranks could be tied to something other than simple &quot;you can effect this,&quot; it could be interesting.<br /> <br /> I still think having glamour be gained by following echoes/taboos related to your kith and faerie tale restrictions would be a good idea, too.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/827-segev","name":"Segev","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1001"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-30T23:40:28-07:00","id":574829,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PookaKnight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/268433-fixing-changeling?p=531944#post531944\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">At that point, you're turning Banality into Changeling: The Lost's Clarity. The primary theme of Changeling: The Dreaming isn't holding on to your sanity. That's Lost's territory. Changeling: The Dreaming is about facing the death of dreams and wonder - the slow erosion of your vital spark.<br /> <br /> Changeling's biggest problem with how it presented Banality was inconsistency. It shows in this thread. It all depends on which sections of which books you read to come away thinking that science somehow equals Banality. In other places, the core book stresses there is nothing inherently banal about science. That's certainly the take I run with, and the game's backstory backs that up.<br /> <br /> Woodstock and Apollo 11's moon landing happened less than a month apart. Woodstock, despite its place in popular culture, is rarely talked about in Changeling. Apollo 11, one of the greatest scientific and engineering achievements of the 20th century, is explicitly the catalyst causing such a massive upsurge in glamour in the world that even the gates to Arcadia opened briefly once more.<br /> <br /> This isn't to say science can't be banal, but so can painting or music composition. It's all about the approach taken not the activity itself.</div> </div> </div> </div>Any sort of solution to Changeling is going to require an explicit acknowledgement to science as being one of the primary drivers of human creativity and dreams. We can do more, feed more, be more with science than we ever could as peasant farmers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/986-mercurial","name":"Mercurial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar986_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-01T05:24:22-07:00","id":575068,"title":"","content":"If we borrow some lore from mage: Technocrats are Banal (Static) not because they use science but because they use science to create a more static *and oppressive* world. Banality and the negative characteristics of the Technocracy seem to coincide. Therefore, Banality would seem to be oppressive stasis rather than science itself. Changelings largely seem to be dynamic beings (although the sluagh are perhaps static/entropic), so it makes sense that excessive stasis would be harmful to them. This is 'mad Weaver' stasis, of course, not what stasis used to be.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-01T07:57:15-07:00","id":575222,"title":"","content":"Or we could uses Mists and Weaving =p","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3075-coridan","name":"Coridan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3075_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"528"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-01T11:46:52-07:00","id":575412,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Coridan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/268433-fixing-changeling?p=575222#post575222\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Or we could uses Mists and Weaving =p</div> </div> </div> </div>Mists and Weaving don't correspond to Glamour and Banality, though. They use a different dualism. CTD at its core is about that duality, which is why DAF is a very different game entirely. DAM, too, uses different systems and dualities than MTA, because the world was a different place back then.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-01T11:49:33-07:00","id":575414,"title":"","content":"When you break both dualities down they are both essentially chaos vs order","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3075-coridan","name":"Coridan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3075_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"528"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}